+Pushing and popping patches
+===========================
+
+``apply`` and ``clean`` act on the whole series. To stop part-way through it, for example to
+test or rework one patch, ``push`` applies the next unapplied patch(es) and ``pop`` reverses
+the most recently applied one(s), in the manner of ``quilt``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    west patch push                          # apply the next patch
+    west patch push my-zephyr-change         # apply up to and including a patch
+    west patch pop -n 2                      # reverse the last two applied patches
+    west patch pop -a                        # reverse everything that was pushed
+
+The applied state is kept in ``.west/patch-stack.json``: ``apply`` skips patches that were
+already pushed, and ``clean`` forgets them. Only patches applied with ``git apply`` (the
+default ``apply-command``) can be popped.
+
+Upstreaming patches
+===================
+
+The ``deps``, ``status``, ``sync`` and ``submit`` sub-commands help to send patches upstream
+and to keep the upstreaming metadata in ``patches.yml`` current. They require the
+`GitHub CLI <https://cli.github.com/>`_ (``gh``) to be installed and authenticated, a git
+remote for the upstream repository (the URL from the manifest), and a git remote for a fork
+of it owned by the authenticated GitHub account. Remotes may be pinned with
+``west config patch.upstream-remote`` and ``west config patch.fork-remote``.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    west patch sync --commit          # record merged pull requests from GitHub
+    west patch deps --write --commit  # refresh dependencies between patches (cached)
+    west patch status                 # what is ready, blocked, or needs a rebase
+    west patch next --path 'kernel/*' # which ready patches to send first
+    west patch submit -n 2            # open up to two draft pull requests
+
+``deps`` derives dependencies textually: for every file a patch fails to apply to on the
+module revision pinned in the manifest, the earlier patches touching that file are
+trial-applied to find the minimal set the patch needs. Results are cached under ``.west/``
+and recorded with ``--write``. ``--dot`` prints the dependency graph in graphviz format.
+
+``status`` fetches the upstream branch and classifies every patch:
+
+===========================  ============================================================
+Status                       Meaning
+===========================  ============================================================
+``ready``                    no unmerged dependency, and applies to the upstream branch
+``blocked``                  a dependency has not been merged upstream yet
+``needs-rebase``             dependencies are satisfied but the patch no longer applies
+``pr-draft``, ``pr-open``    a pull request is recorded and still open
+``pr-closed``                the recorded pull request was closed without merging
+``merged``                   ``merge-status: true``, or GitHub reports the PR merged
+``ignored``                  skipped on purpose via ``custom.upstream.ignore``
+``not-upstreamable``         ``upstreamable: false``
+===========================  ============================================================
+
+``next`` ranks the ready patches by how many other patches they unblock, whether their
+description reads like a bug fix, and their position in the series, and prints size and
+subject area alongside. ``--path`` restricts the candidates to patches touching matching
+files.
+
+``submit`` commits each chosen patch on a branch from the upstream branch (with the author,
+date and a ``Signed-off-by`` trailer), pushes the branch (``patches/<patch-name>``) to the
+fork, opens a **draft** pull request, and records its URL as ``merge-pr``. Only patches whose
+dependencies are already merged upstream are submitted, one pull request per patch unless
+``--group`` is given. Splitting a commit, rewriting the generated message, and marking the
+pull request ready for review are left to the author. ``--dry-run`` builds the branch
+locally and prints the pull request title and body without pushing.
+
+The ``custom`` field of ``patches.yml`` carries the upstreaming metadata:
+
+.. code-block:: yaml
+
+    - path: zephyr/my-zephyr-change.patch
+      # ...
+      custom:
+        upstream:
+          ignore: "superseded by the discussion in issue 12345"  # or: true
+          depends-on:                                              # written by "deps"
+            - zephyr/an-earlier-change.patch
+          manual-depends-on:                                       # written by hand
+            - zephyr/a-change-in-another-file.patch
+          title: "kernel: add a thing"                             # subject override
+          branch: patches/my-zephyr-change                         # written by "submit"
+
+``upstreamable: false`` marks a patch that should never go upstream, whereas ``ignore`` marks
+one that could, but is deliberately skipped for now. ``manual-depends-on`` records
+dependencies ``deps`` cannot see, such as a call into a function another patch adds in a
+different file.
+
